Z||000000||DreXteR||01-08-2001||02:05 PM||||I never hear or read if a Grand National engine fits in to a fiero.  Or is not wise make the swap.<P>DreXteR||||reg||4
Z||000001||Pontiaddict||01-08-2001||03:42 PM||||Basically no, you can make it fit, but a 3800 sc swap would be easier. The 3.8 turbo is set up with a different bellhousing bolt pattern, motor mounts and starter location. Plus the plumbing for the turbo would have to be changed to fit in the Fiero engine bay. You could use a Series 2 3800 block and throw the turbo on it though.||||reg||1
Z||000002||DreXteR||01-08-2001||04:05 PM||||Thanx 4 da info!<P>I ask this 'cause I have a friend that have some contacts with japan.  They bring engines directly from there, maybe his frind have some contacs an can get me the gn engine.<P>BTW, can fit the 300 twin turbo?<P>I dont think so but, maybe there is a possibility.<P>Thanx<P><BR>DreXteR||||reg||1
Z||000003||rockcrawl||01-08-2001||04:24 PM||||Here's a picture of a GN 3.8 turbo in a Fiero engine bay. It happens to have a Countach body kit on it also.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/turbov6.jpg"> <P>It is bolted to a TH425 longitudinal transaxle (Olds Toronado). A TH325 or TH325-4L would also work. More info on my website at <A HREF="http://www.fortunecity.com/silverstone/impala/403/longv8.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fortunecity.com/silverstone/impala/403/longv8.html</A> <P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||hugh||01-08-2001||05:42 PM||||Jon,you are amazing with what you come up with!<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/hugh_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Philphine||01-08-2001||06:20 PM||||is there any weight difference bettween those 3 trannies?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Archie||01-08-2001||06:43 PM||||I designed an adapter system for the GN back in 1991.<P>Here is a picture showing the Adapter plate, FlexPlate & Torque converter mounted to the GN engine.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/GN-swap2.JPG"> <P>Here is a pic. of me bolting something together, notice using new Caddie 440 transmission. Also notice that this is the fit & trim V-8 Archie no gray hair etc.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/GN-swap.JPG"> <P>Here is a pic. of the GN/440 assembly (the one on the right) with a couple of SBC assemblies.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/GN-on-r.JPG"> <P>Please excuse the quality of the pics., they are stills pulled from VHS video.<P>Archie<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||rockcrawl||01-08-2001||09:01 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Jon,you are amazing with what you come up with!<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>That's not my GN powered Countach, It was built/owned by Rick of D&R Replicars. Mine was basically the same car but with a TPI 350 and 325-4L.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>is there any weight difference bettween those 3 trannies?<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>I'm not 100% sure, but I'd bet the TH425 is the heaviest, it is for sure the strongest. The 325 is probably the lightest. The 325-4L is a four speed version of the 325, and is probably the weakest. I had some slippage with the 350/325-4L, but I don't know how many miles were on it when I bought it.<P> I dug up the info on the shop that specializes in the longitudinal conversions, the guys name is Greg at Fiero Concept, 100 W. Wonder St., Wildwood FL 34785, 352-748-7033. Rick gave me that info about 5 years ago, so I'm not sure if it's still good. Greg was very helpful in answering questions when I was doing mine.<P>Archie,<BR> Are you still making those adapters, or was that just a one time thing?<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||6
Z||000008||Will||01-09-2001||09:51 AM||||Use a FWD 3.8 block and do a rebuild with GN HiPo parts. It won't be quite as strong as the longitudinal block, but 500 RWHP should be doable. With the FWD block, you won't need any kind of adapter plate.<P>Turbo plumbing will be a lot of fun either way, though.<p>[This message has been edited by Will (edited 01-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Slammed Fiero||01-09-2001||10:59 AM||||Archie , was the performance of the grand national motor worth all the effort??<P>as for a 300 TT motor fitting , anything fits with money , but with all thats involved with that motor I would stick to GM engines. My friend just rebuilt his 300zx TT motor , it weighs as much as or more than a small block chevy..in the region of 650lbs, plus all the wiring that goes along with the motor , and the expense of rebuilding it. Cost him $4000 to do a stock rebuild, that is for mainly parts on the motor as he is a machinst and does all his own work. <BR><P>------------------<BR>Jonathan M McCreery<BR>86 GT<BR>New Custom White face gauges 17" Tr motorsports Typhoons Ground Control coil overs , Big bars front and rear , Mustang scoop , accel coil , no cat , 4spd , short shift , poly dog bone and bushings momo pedals and shift knob , round headlight conversion , Geni stainless steel oval exhaust tips. Pioneer 6000 CD player , Sony Mobile Amp. Currently doing big brake conversion...||||reg||1
